ok consciousness  and fundamental reality defends Pan  psychism and he's also working at the  moment on a wider public book called  Galileo's error a manifesto for the new  science of consciousness and finally we  have Nicholas Humphrey who is a  psychologist who's done pioneering work  into the evolution of intelligence and  consciousness and his books include the  mind made flesh a history of the mind  and soul dust and the magic of  consciousness so I'm going to invite  each of our speakers to give you their  opening initial pitch on the topic  they'll take four minutes each then  we'll have some discussion we'll go into  a few more themes of this session and  then I will close by inviting questions  from you so Phillip perhaps you could  start and give us your four minutes  worth please okay I'll try my best so my  starting point is we know that  consciousness is real nothing is more  evident than the reality of one's own  feelings and experiences and yet nothing  is harder to integrate into our  scientific worldview it's generally  agreed that we don't really have even  the beginnings of an explanation of how  brains produce consciousness this is the  so called hard problem of consciousness  but although this is all these problems  taking very seriously many people argue  that well if you just look at the great  success of physical science in  explaining more and more of our universe  they sought to give us confidence that  if we just plug away and our standard  methods for investing in the brain will  one day so the problem of consciousness  however a problem of consciousness is  radically unlike any of the other  challenges physical sciences ever faced  the problem is that physical science  works with the purely quantitative  had malaria whereas consciousness is an  essentially qualitative phenomenon just  in the sense that it involves qualities  we think about the redness of a red  experience the sweet smell of flowers  the taste of coffee  you simply can't capture these kind of  qualities in the purely quantitative  language of physical science and indeed  this point was well understood by the  founder of physical science namely  Galileo  so Galileo never intended physical  science to be a complete description of  reality he hoped that it could deal with  the mathematical features of reality but  he took it for granted that it couldn't  capture the qualities of consciousness  which he took to reside in the soul and  moreover I've argued that the reason  physical science has done so well is  because from the start galileo kicks  things off by taking consciousness out  of its domain of inquiry so he gave  physical scientists a more manageable  task so these people who say oh no  physical science has gone really well  it'll one day explain consciousness my  responses it's gone really well  precisely because it was never designed  for that purpose so what about dualism  the view that consciousness is a  property of the immaterial soul outside  of the physical body and brain  unfortunately this view is also hopeless  in my view I think contemporary  neuroscience gives us an incredibly  strong evidence that consciousness is in  the brain rather than the soul so what  we're gonna do when I studied philosophy  and the dying embers of the 20th century  we were taught that these are the only  two options you either think physical  science will explain it in which case  you're a materialist or you think it's  non-physical in which case you do list  fortunately there is a middle way the  view known as punt psychism so rather  than try to explain consciousness in  terms of utterly non conscious brain  processes as we've been trying to do  very little success for many decades now  the pun psychist aspires to explain the  complex consciousness of human and  animal brains in terms of simpler forms  of consciousness simpler forms of  consciousness which are postulated to  exist as basic features of the matter as  basic properties of fundamental  particles so the view feels a bit wacky  a bit new-age but you know I  we should review on it's not on its  cultural associations but on its  explanatory power what pun psychism  offers us is a way of integrating  consciousness into our scientific  picture of the world and it does so in a  way that avoids the deep problems  associated both with physics both with  materialism and with dualism and for  that reason I think at the very least  it's a position we should take very  seriously indeed thanks Philip good  opening pitch Nick would you like to  take on there yes Philip thanks for that  introduction cause it I think you're  absolutely right we need to start by  considering the problem of phenomenal  consciousness of the qualities of  experience but if like me you're sure  that the brain is actually responsible  for producing them then their answer the  question which we were originally posed  can we explain it by examining the brain  I think the answer must in principle be  yes I'm quite sure all the different  scientists were clever enough then by  examining then in what's going on in my  brain right now he or she could in  principle deduce what it's like to be me  at this moment but the problem is of  course that no one is a clever enough to  do that or no we're very far from being  able to deduce consciousness from the  brain so that way of approaching it is I  think a non-starter so the question is  where should we start if not with  neuroscience well I agree with Philip  that we must start with the experience  itself and the phenomenal feel of  consciousness the what it's like to see  read or to feel pain or to taste salt  and we must decide exactly how best to  characterize that experience these  amazing phenomena both subjectively and  so far as possible objectively what are  they leading us to do in the world which  we can look at in measure but then  rather than going straight to the brain  because I think that's wrong way we need  to start theorizing we've got to come up  with some theoretical model which  explains how a physical mechanism of any  kind could in principle the lever  consciousness as we've decided we want  to describe it now I'm not saying that's  going to be easy  in fact we immediately run  against what seems to be an insuperable  problem Phyllis has mentioned it for  surely we won't be describing  consciousness correctly unless we  recognize that it is indeed somehow  immaterial out of this world the  essential feature of consciousness is  that it's like something that couldn't  be delivered by a physical brain as  Colin McGinnis colorfully put it the  brain is just the wrong kind of thing to  give birth to consciousness you might as  well assert that numbers emerge from  biscuits or ethics from rhubarb so well  what should we then do well maybe just  as Phillips has been recommending we are  simply going to have to move the goal  posts and redefine what we mean by a  physical mechanism if we can't get  consciousness from unconscious physical  elements elements as we usually think of  them then maybe yeah I have to say that  the elements are a little bit conscious  to start with or to go back to rhubarb  and or to biscuits and numbers if we  can't get numbers from a numeral this  gets as we know them then biscuits must  be a little bit normally to start with  that's to say that we are going to take  the route of simply postulating the  existence at a small scale of the very  thing were trying to expand  [Applause]  [Music]

